    "HTML1": [
      {
        "json": {
          "html": "<html>\n<head>\n<style>\nbody {\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n  background-color: #f2f2f2;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\n.container {\n  max-width: 800px;\n  margin: 0 auto;\n  padding: 20px;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: #333;\n  font-size: 32px;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  margin-bottom: 10px;\n}\n\np {\n  color: #666;\n  font-size: 18px;\n  line-height: 1.5;\n  margin-bottom: 20px;\n}\n\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"container\">\n<h1>The Etymology of the word spell</h1>\n<p>The word \"<strong>spell</strong>\" has a fascinating etymology that can be traced back to its ancient roots.</p>\n\n<p>The English word \"<strong>spell</strong>\" originated from the Old English word \"<strong>spellian</strong>,\" which meant \"<em>to tell, speak, or relate</em>.\" This Old English term can be traced back to the Proto-Germanic word \"<strong>spellōną</strong>,\" meaning \"<em>to talk, relate, or speak</em>.\"</p>\n\n<p>The Proto-Germanic word \"<strong>spellōną</strong>\" is believed to have its roots in the Proto-Indo-European (PIE) root \"<strong>*spel-</strong>,\" which had the general meaning of \"<em>to say, tell, or speak</em>.\" This PIE root is also the source of other related words in various Indo-European languages, such as the Old High German \"<strong>spellōn</strong>\" (to speak), the Old Norse \"<strong>spjalla</strong>\" (to talk), and the Latin \"<strong>spello</strong>\" (to declare).</p>\n\n<p>Over time, the meaning of the word \"<strong>spell</strong>\" has evolved and expanded. In Old English, \"<strong>spellian</strong>\" referred to the act of speaking or telling a story, but it also came to mean \"<em>to enchant or charm</em>\" in certain contexts. This sense of enchantment or magic is still present in some modern uses of the word \"<strong>spell</strong>,\" such as in the context of casting a spell in witchcraft or sorcery.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to its magical connotations, the word \"<strong>spell</strong>\" also came to be associated with the idea of a sequence of words or letters that had a specific power or effect. This is evident in the usage of \"<strong>spell</strong>\" to refer to a written or spoken word or phrase believed to have magical or supernatural powers, such as a spell in a spellbook or a spell used to ward off evil.</p>\n\n<p>In modern English, the word \"<strong>spell</strong>\" has taken on various meanings. It can refer to a period of time during which a person is under the influence of a particular condition, such as being under a spell of illness or fatigue. It can also refer to the act of correctly arranging the letters of a word, as in spelling a word correctly.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>"
